---
title: Timing & nesting
summary: The schedules that decide when JS runs or HTML arrives, and the one rule for islands inside islands.
related:
  - regions/client-islands
  - regions/server-islands
  - regions/lakes
---

Two small systems cover much ground: **when** a region wakes, and **what occurs** when regions nest. Both are small by intent.

## The schedules

One key, `wake`, carries the schedule for islands (`wake`) and for server islands (`render: 'deferred'`). Thus you learn the words one time:

| Value | When it fires | Good for |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `load` | Immediately after the runtime starts | UI above the fold that must be interactive now |
| `idle` | On `requestIdleCallback` | Widgets that can wait |
| `visible` | When scrolled into the viewport | All content below the fold |
| `interaction` | On the first pointer, key, or focus inside it (islands only) | UI that most visitors never touch |
| `'(media query)'` | When the CSS media query matches | UI for mobile only or desktop only |

On an **island** (`render` defaults to `static`), `wake` says *when the JS runs*. On a **server island** (`render: 'deferred'`), `wake` says *when the HTML is fetched*. The words are the same for both. `interaction` is for islands only. A fetch on interaction would mean interaction with a fallback.

```svelte
<script>
  import Now from '$lib/Now.svelte' with { wake: 'load' };
  import Later from '$lib/Later.svelte' with { wake: 'idle' };
  import Below from '$lib/Below.svelte' with { wake: 'visible' };
  import Phone from '$lib/Phone.svelte' with { wake: '(max-width: 600px)' };
</script>
```

Each of the four demos below is a real island with that schedule:

<script>
  import LoadLive from '$lib/demos/LoadLive.svelte' with { wake: 'load' };
  import IdleLive from '$lib/demos/IdleLive.svelte' with { wake: 'idle' };
  import VisibleLive from '$lib/demos/VisibleLive.svelte' with { wake: 'visible' };
  import MediaLive from '$lib/demos/MediaLive.svelte' with { wake: '(max-width: 600px)' };
</script>

<div class="doc-demo-grid">
  <div class="doc-demo"><LoadLive /></div>
  <div class="doc-demo"><IdleLive /></div>
  <div class="doc-demo"><VisibleLive /></div>
  <div class="doc-demo"><MediaLive /></div>
</div>

### `interaction`: JS only for what people use

`wake: 'interaction'` is the laziest schedule. The island ships **zero JS until a person uses it**. A pointer press, a keystroke, or focus inside the region wakes it. The interaction that woke it is not lost:

- **The first click counts.** ogygia captures it while the island wakes, and replays it when the island is live. A button, a checkbox, or a link activates exactly one time.
- **Typed text stays.** Characters land in the field natively, because it is real HTML. After hydration, the typed value, the selection, and the focus are restored, and `bind:` syncs.
- **Hover warms it.** When the pointer enters the region, the chunk prefetches. Thus, when the press lands, the wake is usually immediate. For an on-demand *hole* (`render: 'deferred', wake: 'interaction'`) the hover fetches and swaps in one go; add `prefetch: 'idle'` to warm its HTML at page idle instead, so the first hover finds it already there (see [`prefetch` in `regions.presets`](/docs/reference/api#ogygiavite-regionspresets)).

One caution: the replayed first event is not a *trusted* browser gesture. Gesture-gated APIs (`window.open`, clipboard, fullscreen) are blocked if called from it. A component can find this condition and adapt:

```svelte
<script>
  import { hydratedBy } from 'ogygia';

  // 'load' | 'idle' | 'visible' | 'interaction' | a media query — or null during SSR.
  // Call during setup, like getContext.
  const woke = hydratedBy();
</script>

{#if woke === 'interaction'}
  <!-- first activation was a replay: render a real <a target="_blank"> instead of window.open -->
{/if}
```

Do not put gesture-gated actions behind `interaction`. All other UI (counters, carts, menus, forms, editors) operates correctly, and costs nothing until a person touches it.

### Tuning `visible`

`visible` uses an IntersectionObserver. Make its trigger margin wider per import, or set a default for the full app:

```svelte
import Chart from '$lib/Chart.svelte' with { wake: 'visible', margin: '200px' };
```

```ts title=vite.config.ts
// default margin for every `visible` island
ogygia({ regions: { visible: { margin: '0px' } } });
```

### How the bytes arrive

The wake decides when an island's code *downloads* as well as when it runs. Nothing to configure:

- **No island code moves before the page has painted.** Every wake except `interaction` starts no earlier than a SvelteKit page would start hydrating: after `DOMContentLoaded` and one painted frame. The page's CSS and hero image have the line to themselves.
- **When an island wakes, its whole graph downloads at once.** The page carries each island's chunk list as data (one small JSON script, every chunk named once). At the wake the runtime adds a `<link rel="modulepreload">` for each chunk the island needs, beside the island's own `import()`. The browser fetches them in parallel, instead of finding each import only after the last file parsed.
- **An island that never wakes downloads nothing.** A `visible` island fetches when it intersects (`regions.visible.margin` is the lead time), an `idle` one in idle time, a media-query one when the query matches, an `interaction` one on the first pointer-over, focus or touch inside it (the hover warm-up, with the waking click replayed). The router's hover prefetch warms the next page's islands the same way.

On a `csr = true` page Kit wakes the islands, not the runtime, so there a `load` island keeps a `fetchpriority="low"` modulepreload hint at the end of the body.

**Props and the page seed come after the content.** Each island's serialized props sit at the end of the body in one `<script type="application/ogygia-props">` per distinct island, keyed by the region's fingerprint, never next to the island. The page seed (`page.data` for islands that read `$page`) comes last, and only when an island on the page actually reads it. A deferred hole's `<link rel="preload" as="fetch">` stays in the head: it fetches content, not code.

**Props do not copy what the seed already carries.** When the seed ships, an island's props are written relative to it: a plain object or array that is also a node of `page.data`, whether the page passed the very same object or a clone of it, becomes a short path into the seed instead of a second copy. The island still receives its own copy of the data when it wakes; nothing it does to its props reaches the seed or another island. A CMS page that hands twelve blocks their slices of one content tree ships the tree once and twelve paths.

**CSS follows the same rule: the render decides.** On a csr=false page the server pass links a stylesheet for each island that actually renders (`<link rel="stylesheet" data-ogygia-region-css>`, one per sheet per request) and nothing for the rest. A marked import the page never places, or the other blocks of a registry that rendered one, cost no stylesheet and no chunk hint. Kit's own links cover the page's static CSS (the page and layout components' styles); island CSS is not in that graph on purpose, so a 300-block registry does not become 300 render-blocking sheets. Kit's `inlineStyleThreshold` governs both channels: a region sheet under it ships as `<style data-ogygia-region-css>` in the head instead of a blocking `<link>` (a hole answer's inlined sheet is hoisted and deduped the same way), so twenty small island sheets cost no requests before first paint.

## Deferred content that is also interactive

A `render: 'deferred'` region is content only. It never ships JS. On it, `wake` is the *fetch* schedule. In the example below, the hole waits until it scrolls into view. Before that, it does not cost a request:

```svelte
import Panel from '$lib/Panel.svelte' with { render: 'deferred', wake: 'visible' };
```

Sometimes a fetched hole *also* needs interactivity. Do not stack two schedules on one import. Nest a normal `wake` island inside the component of the server island. `render` decides how the HTML arrives. `wake` on the inner island decides when its JS runs. See [Server islands → nesting an island](/docs/regions/server-islands).

## Nesting: one rule

Walk up the tree. The **closest marked parent decides**.

| Nesting | What happens |
| --- | --- |
| Island on the page | Gets its own JS |
| Island inside an island | Shares the JS of its parent: one interactive tree, not two |
| Lake inside an island | Stays static HTML. The JS of the lake never ships |
| Island inside a lake | Gets its own JS again |
| Server island inside an island | Renders inline with the parent (`render: 'deferred'` ignored) |

```
page → island → lake → island → …
```

An island in an island shares one tree. Thus the schedule of the child is ignored. It hydrates with its parent. Dev warns when it drops an unnecessary strategy.

## Do / don't

- **Do** set islands below the fold to `visible`. This performance gain has no cost.
- **Do** use `idle` for UI that the user does not touch immediately.
- **Don't** try to make a `render: 'deferred'` region interactive alone. It ships no JS. Nest a `wake` island inside it.


